1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:08,271
The brush clears a gold coin, stamped with a king and figures from several lands: Greek, Iranian and Indian.

2
00:00:08,271 --> 00:00:15,631
The Kushanas ruled across the roads where those lands met. Why would a king put many gods on his coins?

3
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:42,219
How do we know? Kushana gold coins, in Greek letters, are everywhere. The Rabatak inscription in Afghanistan names Kanishka.

4
00:00:42,219 --> 00:00:46,050
And writers such as Ashvaghosha lived at his court.

5
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:10,333
The Kushanas were a branch of the Yuezhi, a people from Central Asia. Kujula Kadphises united the tribes and took the north-west.

6
00:01:10,333 --> 00:01:17,834
His successor Vima Kadphises issued the first Kushana gold coins. The greatest Kushana king was Kanishka.

7
00:01:17,834 --> 00:01:27,604
Tradition dates his accession to 78 CE, the start of the Shaka era. Many modern scholars favour a later date, about 127 CE.

8
00:01:27,604 --> 00:01:36,797
His capital was Purushapura, now Peshawar. He also ruled from Mathura. His empire stretched from Central Asia to the Ganga plains.

9
00:01:36,797 --> 00:01:44,909
Kanishka supported Buddhism. The fourth Buddhist council is said to have met in his time. He built a tall stupa at Peshawar.

10
00:01:44,909 --> 00:01:50,724
His court was home to Ashvaghosha, who wrote the Buddhacharita, and to the physician Charaka.

11
00:01:50,724 --> 00:01:58,905
The Kushanas controlled the Silk Route. Trade in silk, spices and gold passed through their lands, and made them rich.

12
00:02:14,717 --> 00:02:24,151
Scholars disagree. When did Kanishka rule? One view puts the start of his reign in 78 CE, which is why the Shaka era begins then.

13
00:02:24,151 --> 00:02:31,691
Another view places him later, in the early second century, and argues that the Shaka era was founded by someone else.

14
00:02:31,691 --> 00:02:35,229
The coins and inscriptions do not settle the question.

15
00:02:51,050 --> 00:03:00,423
Carve this tip into memory. Kushana: gold, Kanishka, Peshawar. Silk Route. Four anchors for the Kushanas.

16
00:03:00,423 --> 00:03:04,367
Gold, a king, a capital and a road.

17
00:03:20,183 --> 00:03:37,444
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which Kushana king first issued gold coins? The answer: Vima Kadphises. Number two.

18
00:03:37,444 --> 00:03:54,089
Name the capital of Kanishka. The answer: Purushapura, now Peshawar. Number three. Who wrote the Buddhacharita at his court?

19
00:03:54,089 --> 00:04:03,906
The answer: Ashvaghosha. Now one line for Mains. Discuss the role of the Kushanas in trade and culture.

20
00:04:23,717 --> 00:04:33,761
The gold coin goes into its case. It is stamped with many cultures at once. Three facts to take away. Gold, with Greek letters.

21
00:04:33,761 --> 00:04:42,016
Struck in the Kushana empire. Made when the Silk Route was rich. Next gallery: The Satavahanas.

